# ComposeBus Time Service — RAW MONOTONIC base, realized as many time bodies [SPEC:CXC-TIME-SERVICE]

> `time_service` is a generic ComposeBus service app, shaped like `bus_storage` / `fingerprint_storage`: it
> publishes ONE semantic fact — `time/base`, raw monotonic (SETTLED), at a 1 Hz default tick (schedule
> configurable via profile) — and knows nothing about planets, locales, offsets, or rotation. Idle default is
> San Francisco wall clock, and SHIP TIME IS INVARIANT: visiting a planet never changes it. Everything else —
> Caprica, Virgon, Tauron, Kobol, a visited planet's own rotation, the SHIP TIME screen's local-time view — is
> a "time body" that subscribes `time/base` and publishes `time/<body>`, with its own configurable
> presentation/format, the same semantic-realization seam [CXC-VISION-SEMANTIC-REALIZATION] already names for
> widgets. Rotation lives in the game-world entity that owns it, never in time_service
> ([DX-LEAF-OWNS-BEHAVIOR]). `[PLACEMENT UNRATIFIED]` — filed as the next free numbered doc following
> [CXC-VISION-PLACEMENT]'s precedent, pending the architect's own placement ruling. **DRAFT — audit round 1
> applied (`docs/architecture/AUDIT-bus-time-service.md`, [CXC-TIME-AUDIT-R1]): 5 corrections + 2 rulings
> dispositioned. Four forks remain OPEN, now reconciled against the rulings:
> [DD-TIME-WIRE-FORMAT], [DD-TIME-SCHEDULE-RETAINED], [DD-TIME-PROFILE-SHAPE], [DD-TIME-BODY-UNIFICATION].**

---

## 1. Intent & placement

### [CXC-TIME-SERVICE-INTENT] A generic, game-agnostic ComposeBus service app — same shape as `bus_storage` / `fingerprint_storage`

- definition: `time_service` is a standalone ComposeBus service app — an ordinary bus client that originates one semantic fact and nothing else, the same posture `bus_storage` and `fingerprint_storage` already take toward their own contracts. It is GAME-AGNOSTIC: no ComposeIO service app hardcodes a game's vocabulary, and time_service is built to that same discipline.
- shape-precedent: David's own analogy names `services/bus_storage` and `services/fingerprint_storage` as the shape to follow — a lightweight, decentralized service process that owns one contract and publishes it, not a game engine.
- contract: publishes `time/base` at a 1 Hz DEFAULT tick (confirmed by David), raw-monotonic value settled; the tick schedule and the per-body presentation/format are CONFIGURABLE via a game-supplied time PROFILE ([CXC-TIME-GAME-PROFILE]) — see [CXC-TIME-BASE]'s `ruling-c2-q2`. What fields of that profile time_service itself reads (a "base mode" restriction is one candidate, not settled) remains open — [DD-TIME-PROFILE-SHAPE].
- boundary: time_service knows NOTHING of planets, locales, offsets, or rotation. Those are realized by separate "time bodies" ([CXC-TIME-BODY]) and, for planets, by the game-world layer ([CXC-TIME-PLANET-REALIZATION]) — never by time_service itself.
- see-also: [DX-ENGINE-VS-POLICY], [CXC-CONFIG-BUS]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TIME-SERVICE]

### [CXC-TIME-SERVICE-PLACEMENT] Placement unratified

- status: PLACEMENT UNRATIFIED
- definition: filed as a new numbered spec in `corpora/platform-specs`, following [CXC-VISION-PLACEMENT]'s recorded precedent that this directory carries live filename-number collisions and that placement itself is not yet a ruled question. Tag carried in this document's title and here so a query can reach it structurally rather than only in prose.
- see-also: [CXC-VISION-PLACEMENT]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TIME-SERVICE]

---

## 2. The base clock — engine, not policy

### [CXC-TIME-BASE] `time/base` — raw-monotonic value SETTLED; 1 Hz default tick and per-body format are CONFIGURABLE

- status: decided
- definition: time_service publishes `time/base` carrying RAW MONOTONIC time, never a formatted clock string — UTC epoch seconds when idle; game-elapsed-seconds-from-start when a game module is running. This BASE VALUE is SETTLED — raw-monotonic and nothing else — because a planet ([CXC-TIME-PLANET-REALIZATION]) must be able to invert it into its own rotation phase, which a pre-formatted string cannot support.
- tick-schedule: 1 Hz is the DEFAULT publish cadence (David confirmed: "1hz signal is fine", line 17847) — the cadence itself is CONFIGURABLE via the game-supplied time profile ([CXC-TIME-GAME-PROFILE]), not a hard-fixed constant. A profile MAY set a different tick rate for a given game module.
- presentation-configurable: `time/base`'s own payload format is fixed raw-monotonic; any OTHER presentation/format (a formatted clock string, an offset view, a rotation-inverted view) is a per-body REALIZATION concern ([CXC-TIME-BODY], [CXC-TIME-PLANET-REALIZATION]), and that realization's format IS configurable per body/profile.
- ruling-c2-q2: closes audit findings C2 (MISSING) and Q2 (David: "I'm fine with your proposal"). David's line-17810 origin ask — "configurable to emit a time packet on whatever schedule and in whatever format we want" — is CARRIED FORWARD, not superseded: the base VALUE stays raw-monotonic (settled, because a planet must be able to invert it to its own rotation), while the TICK SCHEDULE ([DD-TIME-SCHEDULE-RETAINED]) and the per-body PRESENTATION/FORMAT ([DD-TIME-WIRE-FORMAT]) are the configurable surfaces that honor it. Neither fork is closed by this ruling; both stay OPEN, now explicitly related to the decided configurability rather than orphaned.
- dsd-source: (a local path, not published)
- inference: the requirement that the base VALUE be RAW MONOTONIC rather than a pre-formatted clock remains an ARCHITECT INFERENCE, not David's stated words — derived because a planet must be able to invert a monotonic value into its own rotation phase. Recorded per [DX-INTERPRETATION-IS-AN-INFERENCE], not promoted beyond that.
- rule: time_service never emits a per-body value on `time/base` itself — that is what [CXC-TIME-BODY] and [CXC-TIME-PLANET-REALIZATION] exist to do, each on its own `time/<body>` namespace.
- see-also: [DX-ENGINE-VS-POLICY], [DX-STATE-ON-BUS], [CXC-CONFIG-BUS], [DD-TIME-WIRE-FORMAT], [DD-TIME-SCHEDULE-RETAINED], [DD-TIME-PROFILE-SHAPE]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TIME-SERVICE]

### [CXC-TIME-SHIP-INVARIANT] Ship time is invariant — idle default is San Francisco wall clock

- idle-default: not playing a game -> ship time = real wall clock in San Francisco.
- rule: ship time NEVER changes on proximity to a planet or locale. Only a game-supplied profile, loaded at game START ([CXC-TIME-GAME-PROFILE]), may define a different ship-time offset — never a runtime proximity effect.
- see-also: [CXC-TIME-BASE], [CXC-TIME-GAME-PROFILE]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TIME-SERVICE]

---

## 3. Time bodies — the semantic realization

### [CXC-TIME-BODY] Everything else is a "time body" realizing `time/base` — ARCHITECT INFERENCE

- inference: unifying locale-offset and planetary-rotation into ONE "time body" shape — `{offset, rotation (default 24h), format, proximity-gated?}` — is the architect's proposal, not David's stated words. Recorded per [DX-INTERPRETATION-IS-AN-INFERENCE] and left as an open fork rather than a settled model; see [DD-TIME-BODY-UNIFICATION].
- definition: a time body SUBSCRIBES `time/base` and PUBLISHES `time/<body>`. A clock leaf subscribes to a body's namespace and displays it. No device is aware of any other body — the same simultaneous-realization pattern [CXC-VISION-SEMANTIC-REALIZATION] already names for widgets, applied here to time.
- see-also: [CXC-VISION-SEMANTIC-REALIZATION], [DX-STATE-ON-BUS], [DD-TIME-BODY-UNIFICATION]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TIME-SERVICE]

### [CXC-TIME-IDLE-LOCALES] Idle locale table — Caprica/Virgon/Tauron real-world stand-ins; Kobol reserved

- status: decided
- ruling-q1: DECIDED, David-confirmed (audit finding Q1) — Vietnam is a single time zone (Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh, UTC+7), so any city within it is equivalent and the country-to-city resolution is not an unflagged architect pick. Ho Chi Minh City stands as the idle locale, ambiguity hedge removed.
- dsd-source: (a local path, not published)
- locale-caprica: Copenhagen
- locale-virgon: Singapore
- locale-tauron: Ho Chi Minh City, Vietnam (Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh) — decided, audit Q1.
- locale-kobol: intentionally NOT used in idle — reserved for gameplay, per David's own words.
- rule: this table governs the IDLE realization only. An in-game profile may define different offsets for the same body names, per [CXC-TIME-GAME-PROFILE].
- see-also: [CXC-TIME-BODY], [CXC-TIME-SHIP-INVARIANT]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TIME-SERVICE]

### [CXC-TIME-GAME-PROFILE] In-game — a time profile defines unique per-body offsets, loaded at game start

- rule: the profile is loaded ONCE, at game start. Which fields it carries, and which layer (time_service vs. a time body vs. the game-world layer) consumes each, is not yet settled — see [DD-TIME-PROFILE-SHAPE], which now also carries the "base mode" framing as ONE CANDIDATE profile shape (audit C3), not a settled restriction.
- see-also: [CXC-CONFIG-BUS], [CXC-TIME-SHIP-INVARIANT], [DD-TIME-PROFILE-SHAPE]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TIME-SERVICE]

---

## 4. Planetary rotation — game-world layer, never time_service

### [CXC-TIME-PLANET-REALIZATION] A planet subscribes `time/base` and publishes `time/<planet>` by its own rotation, IFF Galactica is nearby

- rule: rotation period is a property of the PLANET, not of time_service. The planet — a game-world entity, not time_service — subscribes `time/base`, and IFF Galactica is nearby, publishes `time/<planet>` inverted through its own rotation period (default 24h absent a game-specific value). The 24h default is an ARCHITECT INFERENCE — an unconfirmed proposal, not a value David specified — see [DD-TIME-BODY-UNIFICATION], which flags the same default for the design-input audit rather than settling it.
- proximity-gate: a planet publishes its own time body only while Galactica is nearby; time_service performs no proximity check of any kind and holds no notion of "nearby."
- zero-new-time-service-code: this is the O(0) shape [DX-A-NEW-FACT-IS-STATE-NEVER-A-HANDLER] requires — time_service publishes the fact once; it is never edited, and never grows a special case, to add a new planet's rotation.
- ownership: the cheapest node that can own rotation behavior is the planet entity itself, per [DX-LEAF-OWNS-BEHAVIOR] — rotation NEVER lifts into time_service as a coordinator concern.
- see-also: [DX-LEAF-OWNS-BEHAVIOR], [DX-A-NEW-FACT-IS-STATE-NEVER-A-HANDLER], [CXC-TIME-BODY], [CXC-TIME-BASE]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TIME-SERVICE]

---

## 5. The Ship Time view — local-time selector, nested in CHRONO

### [CXC-TIME-VIEW-SELECTOR] "Local Time" — a locale selector on the Ship Time screen; selecting a view never alters ship time

- definition: the SHIP TIME leaf ([CXC-MODE-NAV-CIC-CHRONO-INSTANCE]'s `ship-time` slug) carries a locale SELECTOR that picks which body's time to VIEW — Ship / Local Time (an alias resolving, proximity-gated, to the nearby body) / named worlds (Kobol, Caprica, ...).
- rule: selecting a view NEVER writes or alters ship time itself ([CXC-TIME-SHIP-INVARIANT]) — the selector changes what is DISPLAYED, never what time_service publishes on `time/base`.
- definition-continued: nests in [CXC-MODE-NAV-CIC-CHRONO-INSTANCE]'s already-ratified SHIP TIME leaf.
- see-also: [CXC-MODE-NAV-CIC-CHRONO-INSTANCE], [CXC-TIME-BODY], [CXC-TIME-SHIP-INVARIANT]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TIME-SERVICE]

