## 6. Open forks — not yet ruled

### [DD-TIME-WIRE-FORMAT] `time/base` wire format — epoch vs. ISO-8601-UTC

- decision: OPEN — should `time/base`'s payload be a numeric UNIX epoch or an ISO-8601-UTC string?
- status: raised
- alternatives: epoch (numeric UNIX seconds); ISO-8601-UTC (string)
- rationale: the architect leans epoch — a raw numeric monotonic value is the cheapest form for a time body to invert into its own offset or rotation phase, and matches [CXC-TIME-BASE]'s raw-monotonic requirement most directly. Not David's ruling.
- ruling-context: David's line-17810 "configurable... format" ask is honored at the per-body PRESENTATION layer, per [CXC-TIME-BASE]'s `ruling-c2-q2` — this fork is the narrower, still-open question of which single wire encoding time_service itself uses for the raw value, not the general format-configurability question.
- dsd-source: (a local path, not published)
- invalidating-assumption: reopens if a consuming leaf needs a human-legible wire value with no client-side conversion available.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TIME-SERVICE]

### [DD-TIME-SCHEDULE-RETAINED] 1 Hz default publish plus a retained snapshot for late joiners?

- decision: OPEN — beyond the DEFAULT 1 Hz cadence (now confirmed CONFIGURABLE per profile, [CXC-TIME-BASE]'s `tick-schedule`), should time_service ALSO retain its last `time/base` value so a leaf joining mid-tick reads a value immediately rather than waiting up to one tick period?
- status: raised
- alternatives: publish-only, no retained snapshot; publish plus a retained/last-value snapshot
- rationale: not ruled — raised because [CXC-CONFIG-BUS] and other ComposeBus services already use retained-value patterns for late joiners, but David has not confirmed this applies to `time/base`.
- ruling-context: David's line-17810 "configurable... schedule" ask is now honored — the TICK RATE itself is a configurable profile field, per [CXC-TIME-BASE]'s `ruling-c2-q2`. This fork is the narrower, still-open question of a retained-snapshot mechanism, not the schedule-configurability question itself.
- dsd-source: (a local path, not published)
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TIME-SERVICE]

### [DD-TIME-PROFILE-SHAPE] What the game time profile carries, and who consumes which part

- decision: OPEN — the full shape of the game-supplied time profile ([CXC-TIME-GAME-PROFILE]) and which fields time_service reads versus which fields a time body or the game-world layer reads.
- status: raised
- alternatives: a single flat profile time_service reads in full; a profile split by consumer, with a "base mode" field scoped to time_service and per-body offsets/rotation defaults scoped to time bodies and the game-world layer.
- rationale: not ruled in full — David's ruling (audit C3: "Yes, base mode should be a candidate") confirms the "time_service reads only a base-mode field" shape is a legitimate CANDIDATE for this fork, not that it is settled; [CXC-TIME-BASE] and [CXC-TIME-GAME-PROFILE] no longer assert it as fact. The full profile shape and field-routing question remains OPEN.
- dsd-source: (a local path, not published)
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TIME-SERVICE]

### [DD-TIME-BODY-UNIFICATION] Whether locale-offset and planetary-rotation truly unify into one "time body" shape

- decision: OPEN — is `{offset, rotation (default 24h), format, proximity-gated?}` the right single shape for BOTH an idle locale (Copenhagen, Singapore, Ho Chi Minh City) and an in-game planet's own rotation, or do they need distinct models?
- status: raised
- alternatives: one unified "time body" shape for both locales and planets (architect proposal, [CXC-TIME-BODY]); two distinct shapes — a simple offset-only locale realization and a separate rotation-bearing planet realization
- rationale: this unification is an ARCHITECT INFERENCE per [DX-INTERPRETATION-IS-AN-INFERENCE], not something David stated. Flagged for the design-input audit rather than resolved here.
- invalidating-assumption: reopens if a planet needs fields (e.g. an orbital-phase-dependent format) an idle locale never needs, which would break a single shared shape.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TIME-SERVICE]
- david-ruling: DECIDED = UNIFIED (David 2026-08-19). A time body is a first-class TIME-OBJECT with its OWN time source; the idle-locale offset (UTC + IANA tz) is the DEGENERATE case, not the model. Confirmed by David's generality test: two ships — Galactica AND Pegasus — must each run on their OWN time base, which the unified object model gives directly (each publishes time/<object> off its own source) and a singleton special-cased time/ship could not. Consequences: bodies are OBJECT-NAMED (time/<object>); SHIP TIME becomes a VIEW resolving to the relevant ship-object (the current console's ship), not a fixed namespace. time_service realizes the idle-locale (offset) source; the game-world layer realizes the object-with-its-own-base source (Galactica, Pegasus, planet rotation) at the SAME seam. This ruling resolves QA run-1 notes 1 (body wire format) and 2 (idle bodies emit nonsense in game mode) as symptoms of the offset-only stopping point. dsd-source-followon: docs/architecture/AUDIT-bus-time-service.md + composeplatform-qa run 2026-08-19.

